# III. Knowledge Architecture

## A. Markdown Knowledge Base

The primary knowledge representation uses Markdown files organized within a controlled knowledge repository.

This provides:

- Human-readable source material
- Simple organization
- Version control
- Explicit knowledge inclusion
- Separation from generated application content

The knowledge repository functions as a curated source layer rather than an unrestricted document collection.

## B. Knowledge Eligibility

A file should not automatically become AI knowledge simply because the system can access it.

Knowledge eligibility is an explicit architectural concept.

```text
File
 │
 ├── Approved as Knowledge ──► Retrieval System
 │
 ├── Generated / Temporary ──► Not Automatically Knowledge
 │
 ├── Protected ──────────────► Authorization Required
 │
 └── No Access ──────────────► Excluded
```

This prevents accidental expansion of the assistant's knowledge scope.

## C. Retrieval and Grounding

When a user asks a question, the system retrieves relevant authorized source material before generating the response.

The retrieved material becomes part of the model context.

The model can then:

- Interpret source material
- Summarize information
- Answer questions
- Combine information from multiple authorized sources
- Identify supporting sources

The model is not treated as a replacement for the underlying source documents.

