# IV. Authorization Architecture

## A. Permission Model

The system defines four primary permission levels:

| Permission | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `READ_ONLY` | Information can be read but not modified |
| `READ_WRITE` | Authorized information can be read and modified |
| `PROTECTED` | Restricted information requiring additional authorization |
| `NO_ACCESS` | Information must not be accessed |

## B. Access Enforcement

Authorization should be enforced outside the language model wherever possible.

The intended pattern is:

```text
User
 │
 ▼
Identity / Role
 │
 ▼
Permission Evaluation
 │
 ├── Authorized ──► Retrieve
 │
 └── Unauthorized ─► Deny
```

The language model should not be the sole mechanism used to determine whether a user can access a resource.

## C. Protected Information

The architecture recognizes categories of information that may require stronger protection in a school environment.

Examples include:

- Student records
- Individual education information
- Disciplinary information
- Health-related information
- Personnel records
- Legal information
- Restricted administrative information

No real protected institutional records are included in the public repository.

